+\section{Bounded-window prime-tail compression and finite radical certificates}
+\label{vfourB:sec}\label{vfourB:section}
+
+This section integrates the bounded-window construction of
+\cite{vfourLowWindow}. Its purpose is a quantitative finite-rank
+approximation of the actual complete prime tail, followed by an exact
+finite weighted-Gram certificate format. It does not assign a sign to the
+low window. The separate all-Schatten result in
+Theorem~\ref{vfourD:schatten} gives the stronger negative-spectral-count
+input; its proof is not repeated here.
+
+\subsection{The centered operator, conventions, and complete tail}
+\label{vfourB:setting}
+
+We use the actual spaces, kernel, and forms of
+\eqref{a:eq:weights}--\eqref{a:eq:X}. Throughout this section inner products
+are \emph{linear in the first argument}, as in the common notation:
+\[
+ \ip{u}{v}_{\Hh}=\int_{\R}u(x)\overline{v(x)}\,\frac{dx}{W(x)},
+ \qquad \ip{a}{b}_{\C^n}=\sum_{k=1}^n a_k\overline{b_k}.
+\]
+In particular, if $Z:\C^n\to\Hh_c$ has columns $z_k$, then
+$(Z^*u)_k=\ip{u}{z_k}_{\Hh}$ and
+$(Z^*Z)_{ab}=\ip{z_b}{z_a}_{\Hh}$. All adjoints and Gram matrices below
+use this convention.
+
+Let $\mathsf C u=u-2m(u)\kappa$ be the weighted orthogonal projection
+in~\eqref{a:eq:centering}. Write $L_c$ for the self-adjoint representative
+of $q_0$ on $X_c\subset\Hh_c$, and set
+$D_c=\mathsf C D_b|_{\Hh_c}$, where $D_b$ is the complete bounded
+correction of~\eqref{eq:Db-physical}. Thus
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:centered-form}
+ q_0[u]=\tg[u]-\ip{D_cu}{u}_{\Hh},\qquad
+ \norm{D_c}\le d,\qquad L_c\ge-dI.
+\end{equation}
+Lemma~\ref{a:lem:prime} permits the explicit choice $d=10$.
+The correction contains $\cg\norm u_2^2$ and every prime-power shift;
+$D_c$ is not the operator called $B$ in~\eqref{eq:Db-physical}.
+Lemma~\ref{a:lem:fourier} gives
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:gamma}
+ \tg[u]=\int_{\R}d_\Gamma(t)|\widehat u(t)|^2\,dt,
+ \qquad d_\Gamma(t)\ge\tfrac12\log(1+4t^2).
+\end{equation}
+Here the Fourier transform is the ordinary unitary transform.
+The logarithmic large-frequency growth also follows from the digamma
+formulas in \cite[Sections 5.9 and 5.11]{DLMF}; the explicit inverse cutoff
+below uses only the displayed lower bound.
+
+By Lemma~\ref{a:lem:radical}, the linearly independent vectors
+$r_j=\kappa^{(2j)}-4^{-j}\kappa$ are full-domain radicals. Put
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:known-radical}
+ \Nn=\overline{\operatorname{span}\{r_j:j\ge1\}}^{\Hh},
+ \qquad \Kk=\Hh_c\ominus\Nn,
+ \qquad P_0=\one_{\{0\}}(L_c).
+\end{equation}
+The representation theorem and closedness imply
+$\Nn\subseteq\ker L_c$ and $L_cP_\Nn=0$. We do not identify
+$P_\Nn$ with $P_0$.
+
+For an integer $M\ge1$, define the actual infinite prime tail by
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:tail-form}
+ p_{>M}[u]=2\operatorname{Re}\sum_{n>M}
+ a_n\int_{\R}u(x+\log n)\overline{u(x)}\,dx
+ =\ip{D_Mu}{u}_{\Hh},\qquad u\in\Hh_c.
+\end{equation}
+Its bounded self-adjoint representative $D_M$ is the centered raw weighted
+row, with both orientations paired. We record the complete-tail caps used
+in \cite{vfourLowWindow}:
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:tail-caps}
+ \begin{gathered}
+ W(x)\le40e^{4|x|-\pi e^{2|x|}},\qquad
+ W_*:=\norm W_\infty\le40e^{-\pi},\\
+ \norm{D_M}\le\tau_M:=80\sum_{n>M}\Lambda(n)n^{3/2}e^{-\pi n}
+ \le160(M+1)^2e^{-\pi(M+1)}.
+ \end{gathered}
+\end{equation}
+These are weighted-space norm bounds on the \emph{full} tail, not a
+positivity statement.
+
+For completeness, the constant $40$ follows from the envelope in
+Lemma~\ref{a:lem:theta}. In its defining series, the terms
+$j^4e^{-\pi(j^2-1)}$ for $j\ge2$ have successive ratio at most
+$(3/2)^4e^{-5\pi}<1/1000$, and the first is less than $1/500$.
+Their sum is therefore less than $1/400$. Using $\pi<22/7$ gives
+$C_\kappa<(1936/49)(401/400)<40$.
+For a displacement $s\ge0$, the same weighted-shift argument as in
+Lemma~\ref{a:lem:prime} gives
+\[
+ \sup_x\sqrt{W(x)W(x+s)}\le40e^{2s-\pi e^s}.
+\]
+Consequently each paired $n$th row has norm at most
+$80\Lambda(n)n^{3/2}e^{-\pi n}$. Centering is contractive.
+Finally $\log n\le\sqrt n$ for $n\ge1$, and the consecutive ratios of
+$n^2e^{-\pi n}$ for $n\ge2$ are less than $1/2$. Summing that geometric
+majorant proves the last inequality in~\eqref{vfourB:tail-caps}.
+In every upper bound or parameter choice below, the explicit cap
+$40e^{-\pi}$ may replace $W_*$.
+
+\subsection{Finite-rank approximation on a bounded spectral window}
+\label{vfourB:approximation}
+
+Fix a bounded Borel set $J\subset[-R,R]$, where $R\ge0$, and write
+$P_J=\one_J(L_c)$. The spectral theorem gives
+$\Ran P_J\subset\dom L_c$ and
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:window-energy}
+ \tg[u]\le(R+d)\norm u_{\Hh}^2,
+ \qquad \norm u_2\le\sqrt{W_*}\norm u_{\Hh}
+ \qquad(u\in\Ran P_J).
+\end{equation}
+For integers $N\ge M$, define the finite raw sum
+\[
+ D_{(M,N]}^{\mathrm{raw}}u(x)
+ =W(x)\sum_{M<n\le N}a_n
+       \bigl(u(x+\log n)+u(x-\log n)\bigr).
+\]
+An individual oriented row maps ordinary $L^2(dx)$ into the ambient
+weighted space $L^2(dx/W)$, since
+\[
+ \norm{W(\cdot)u(\cdot+s)}_{L^2(dx/W)}^2
+ =\int_{\R}W(x)|u(x+s)|^2\,dx\le W_*\norm u_2^2.
+\]
+Pairing the orientations preserves evenness, after which $\mathsf C$ is
+applied. Thus
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:finite-shift-cap}
+ \norm{\mathsf C D_{(M,N]}^{\mathrm{raw}}}
+       _{\Le\to\Hh_c}
+ \le C_{M,N}:=2\sqrt{W_*}\sum_{M<n\le N}\frac{\Lambda(n)}{\sqrt n}.
+\end{equation}
+Only this \emph{finite} shift sum is used as an ordinary-to-weighted map.
+The infinite remainder is bounded in the full $\Hh$ norm by $\tau_N$;
+no $L^2(dx)\to\Hh$ bound is asserted for the infinite prime tail.
+
+Let $F_T$ be the ordinary Fourier projection onto $[-T,T]$, and set
+\[
+ W_B=\sup_{|x|>B}W(x),\qquad
+ \nu(T)=\inf_{|t|>T}d_\Gamma(t).
+\]
+For $B,T>0$ and an integer $m\ge1$, define
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:taylor-map}
+ \begin{split}
+ \mathcal T_{B,T,m}u(x)
+ &=\one_{[-B,B]}(x)\sum_{\substack{0\le k<m\\k\ \mathrm{even}}}
+       x^k\ell_k(u),\\
+ \ell_k(u)&=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}\,k!}
+            \int_{-T}^T(it)^k\widehat u(t)\,dt.
+ \end{split}
+\end{equation}
+The output is used only in ordinary $L^2$, and the map has rank at most
+$\lceil m/2\rceil$. Define
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:error-parameters}
+ \zeta_{B,T,m}=\sqrt{\frac{2BT}{\pi}}\frac{(BT)^m}{m!},\qquad
+ b_{R;B,T,m}=\sqrt{W_B}
+       +\sqrt{\frac{R+d}{\nu(T)}}+\sqrt{W_*}\,\zeta_{B,T,m}.
+\end{equation}
+
+\begin{theorem}[Actual bounded-window tail approximation]
+\label{vfourB:approximation-theorem}
+Assume $\nu(T)>0$, and put
+\[
+ A=\mathsf C D_{(M,N]}^{\mathrm{raw}}
+                   \mathcal T_{B,T,m}|_{\Hh_c},\qquad
+ E=\tau_N+C_{M,N}b_{R;B,T,m}.
+\]
+Then $A$ is bounded on $\Hh_c$, has rank at most $\lceil m/2\rceil$, and
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:row-error}
+ \norm{(D_M-A)P_J}\le E.
+\end{equation}
+For the self-adjoint finite-rank operator $S=(A+A^*)/2$, one has the
+\emph{compressed} estimate
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:compressed-error}
+ \norm{P_J(D_M-S)P_J}\le E.
+\end{equation}
+No corresponding estimate for the symmetrized row $(D_M-S)P_J$ is claimed.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+For $u\in\Ran P_J$, the spatial and Fourier tails satisfy
+\[
+ \norm{\one_{\{|x|>B\}}u}_2\le\sqrt{W_B}\norm u_{\Hh},\qquad
+ \norm{(I-F_T)u}_2
+ \le\sqrt{\frac{R+d}{\nu(T)}}\norm u_{\Hh}.
+\]
+The integral Taylor remainder for real $tx$ is bounded by
+$|tx|^m/m!$. The resulting kernel error on
+$[-B,B]\times[-T,T]$, including the factor $(2\pi)^{-1/2}$, has
+Hilbert--Schmidt norm at most $\zeta_{B,T,m}$. The odd terms vanish on even
+inputs. Decomposing
+\[
+ u-\mathcal T_{B,T,m}u
+ =\one_{\{|x|>B\}}u+\one_{[-B,B]}(u-F_Tu)
+   +\bigl(\one_{[-B,B]}F_Tu-\mathcal T_{B,T,m}u\bigr)
+\]
+and using~\eqref{vfourB:window-energy} therefore gives
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:ordinary-error}
+ \norm{u-\mathcal T_{B,T,m}u}_2
+ \le b_{R;B,T,m}\norm u_{\Hh}.
+\end{equation}
+All functionals $\ell_k$ are bounded on $\Hh$ by its continuous embedding
+in ordinary $L^2$, so $A$ is bounded and its rank has the stated bound.
+The identity
+\[
+ D_M-A=D_N+\mathsf C D_{(M,N]}^{\mathrm{raw}}
+                         (I-\mathcal T_{B,T,m})|_{\Hh_c}
+\]
+combines the complete weighted tail estimate with
+\eqref{vfourB:finite-shift-cap} and~\eqref{vfourB:ordinary-error} to
+prove~\eqref{vfourB:row-error}. Finally
+$P_J(D_M-S)P_J$ is the self-adjoint part of $P_J(D_M-A)P_J$.
+Taking a self-adjoint part cannot increase the operator norm, proving
+\eqref{vfourB:compressed-error} without estimating an uncompressed
+adjoint row.
+\end{proof}
+
+The columns of $A$ are centered finite prime-shift rows of the ordinary
+compactly supported functions $\one_{[-B,B]}x^k$. Their construction does
+not require a sharp spectral projection. The explicit schedule below
+makes $E$ arbitrarily small; hence $D_MP_J$ is compact, even when $P_J$
+has infinite rank.
+
+\subsection{Explicit inverse-Gamma cutoff and the minimal rank schedule}
+\label{vfourB:schedule}
+
+Fix $0<\varepsilon\le1$ and set
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:integer-cutoff}
+ N=\max\{M,\lceil3+\log(4/\varepsilon)\rceil\}.
+\end{equation}
+Then $\tau_N<\varepsilon/4$. Indeed the function
+$160y^2e^{-(\pi-1)y-4}$ decreases for $y\ge4$ and is less than one at
+$y=4$, so~\eqref{vfourB:tail-caps} with
+$y=N+1\ge4+\log(4/\varepsilon)$ suffices.
+If $C_{M,N}=0$, take $A=0$, with no further parameters required.
+Otherwise choose an explicit cap $C_\sharp\ge C_{M,N}>0$ and define
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:spatial-cutoff}
+ X_\varepsilon=\max\{e^2,4C_\sharp\sqrt{40}/\varepsilon\},\qquad
+ Y_\varepsilon=\frac4\pi\log X_\varepsilon,\qquad
+ B=\tfrac12\log Y_\varepsilon.
+\end{equation}
+The inverse of the logarithmic Gamma lower bound gives the explicit
+frequency cutoff
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:inverse-gamma}
+ H_\varepsilon=
+ \max\left\{1,\frac{16C_\sharp^2(R+d)}{\varepsilon^2}\right\},\qquad
+ T=\frac12\sqrt{e^{2H_\varepsilon}-1}.
+\end{equation}
+In particular $\nu(T)\ge H_\varepsilon>0$, including when $R+d=0$.
+Choose $m$ to be the \emph{smallest positive integer} satisfying
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:minimal-m}
+ \begin{split}
+ m&\ge4eBT,\\
+ m&\ge\max\left\{0,
+   \log_4\!\left(
+       \frac{4C_\sharp\sqrt{W_*}\sqrt{2BT/\pi}}{\varepsilon}
+           \right)\right\}.
+ \end{split}
+\end{equation}
+Equivalently, $m$ is the maximum of $1$ and the ceilings of the two
+right-hand sides.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Complete error budget and rank cost]
+\label{vfourB:rank-schedule}
+The preceding choices give $E\le\varepsilon$ in
+Theorem~\ref{vfourB:approximation-theorem}. With the specified minimal $m$
+and the explicit cap
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:chebyshev-cap}
+ C_\sharp=2\sqrt{W_*}\,[4(\log2)\sqrt N+5],
+\end{equation}
+for fixed $M,R,d,W_*$ the construction satisfies
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:rank-bound}
+ \rank A\le\lceil m/2\rceil
+ \le\exp\!\left(O_{M,R,d,W_*}
+       \bigl(\varepsilon^{-2}[1+\log(1/\varepsilon)]\bigr)\right).
+\end{equation}
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+For $X_\varepsilon\ge e^2$ one has
+$Y_\varepsilon\le X_\varepsilon$. The decreasing theta envelope gives
+\[
+ C_\sharp\sqrt{W_B}
+ \le C_\sharp\sqrt{40}\,Y_\varepsilon
+                  e^{-\pi Y_\varepsilon/2}
+ \le\frac{C_\sharp\sqrt{40}}{X_\varepsilon}
+ \le\frac{\varepsilon}{4}.
+\]
+Equation~\eqref{vfourB:inverse-gamma} gives
+$C_\sharp\sqrt{(R+d)/\nu(T)}\le\varepsilon/4$.
+The elementary factorial estimate $m!\ge(m/e)^m$ and
+\eqref{vfourB:minimal-m} give
+\[
+ \zeta_{B,T,m}\le\sqrt{2BT/\pi}\,4^{-m},\qquad
+ C_\sharp\sqrt{W_*}\,\zeta_{B,T,m}\le\varepsilon/4.
+\]
+Together with the discarded complete tail, these are the four parts of
+$E\le\varepsilon$.
+
+To check~\eqref{vfourB:chebyshev-cap}, write
+$\psi_{\mathrm C}(x)=\sum_{n\le x}\Lambda(n)$.
+The central-binomial estimate yields
+$\psi_{\mathrm C}(n)\le\psi_{\mathrm C}(\lceil n/2\rceil)+n\log2$
+for integers $n\ge2$. Iterating down to $1$ gives, for real $x\ge1$,
+\[
+ \psi_{\mathrm C}(x)\le2(\log2)x+\log x+\log2.
+\]
+Partial summation then gives
+\[
+ \sum_{n\le N}\frac{\Lambda(n)}{\sqrt n}
+ =\frac{\psi_{\mathrm C}(N)}{\sqrt N}
+   +\frac12\int_1^N\frac{\psi_{\mathrm C}(t)}{t^{3/2}}\,dt
+ \le4(\log2)\sqrt N+5,
+\]
+which proves the cap. As $\varepsilon\downarrow0$,
+$N=O_M(1+\log(1/\varepsilon))$ and
+$C_\sharp^2=O_{M,W_*}(1+\log(1/\varepsilon))$.
+Hence
+$H_\varepsilon=O_{M,R,d,W_*}
+(\varepsilon^{-2}[1+\log(1/\varepsilon)])$,
+$T\le e^{H_\varepsilon}/2$, and $B$ grows at most logarithmically in
+$1+\log(1/\varepsilon)$. The logarithmic second requirement
+in~\eqref{vfourB:minimal-m} is $O(H_\varepsilon+1+\log(1/\varepsilon))$.
+Taking the \emph{smallest} integer satisfying both requirements now gives
+\eqref{vfourB:rank-bound}; enlarging constants covers the remaining compact
+range of $\varepsilon$.
+\end{proof}
+
+An arbitrarily inflated cap $C_\sharp$, or an arbitrarily enlarged
+admissible $m$, is not covered by the stated rank estimate. The construction
+is not claimed to be optimal or practical. In particular, the exponential
+$e^{2H_\varepsilon}$ in~\eqref{vfourB:inverse-gamma} cannot be replaced by
+$e^{H_\varepsilon}$ when inverting the displayed Gamma bound.
+
+\subsection{Continuous filters and exact finite constrained Grams}
+\label{vfourB:gram-section}
+
+Let $I\subset J$, and choose a real continuous function $0\le\phi\le1$
+that equals one on $I$ and has support in $J$, whenever such a filter is
+available. Put
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:filtered-operator}
+ F=\phi(L_c),\qquad S_\phi=\tfrac12 F(A+A^*)F.
+\end{equation}
+Since $FP_J=F=P_JF$, the compressed estimate implies the global bound
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:filtered-error}
+ \norm{FD_MF-S_\phi}\le E.
+\end{equation}
+Thus $F$ is the functional calculus of the \emph{complete} centered
+operator, not of a Gamma-only substitute.
+
+Let $r=\lceil m/2\rceil$ and index its columns by the even integers
+$0\le k<m$. Set
+\[
+ y_k=\mathsf C D_{(M,N]}^{\mathrm{raw}}
+                     (\one_{[-B,B]}x^k),\qquad
+ g_k=\mathsf C(Wb_k),\qquad
+ \widehat b_k(t)=\frac{(-it)^k}{\sqrt{2\pi}\,k!}\one_{[-T,T]}(t).
+\]
+Here $b_k\in\Le$, $Wb_k\in\Hh$, and Plancherel gives
+$\ell_k(u)=\ip{u}{g_k}_{\Hh}$ for $u\in\Hh_c$ under the declared
+linear-first convention. Consequently, with column maps
+$Y,G:\C^r\to\Hh_c$, one has $A=YG^*$. Define
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:factorization}
+ Z=(FY,FG):\C^{2r}\to\Hh_c,\qquad
+ H_0=\frac12\begin{pmatrix}0&I_r\\ I_r&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
+ S_\phi=ZH_0Z^*.
+\end{equation}
+If $A=0$ was chosen above, take empty column maps instead.
+
+Let $R_j:\C^j\to\Hh_c$ have columns $r_1,\ldots,r_j$, and let $P_j$
+be the true $\Hh$ projection onto their span. Put
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:constraint-gram}
+ \begin{gathered}
+ H_j=R_j^*R_j>0,\qquad B_j=R_j^*Z,\qquad
+ P_j=R_jH_j^{-1}R_j^*,\qquad Q_j=I-P_j,\\
+ G_j=Z^*Q_jZ=Z^*Z-B_j^*H_j^{-1}B_j\ge0.
+ \end{gathered}
+\end{equation}
+For $j=0$ the constraint term is absent. The entries of these matrices
+are the actual reciprocal-theta weighted inner products. In particular,
+$G_j$ is not a Euclidean Gram of unweighted coefficients.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Finite known-radical tail certificates]
+\label{vfourB:gram-certificate}
+Define
+\[
+ K_j=G_j^{1/2}H_0G_j^{1/2},\qquad
+ \alpha_j^+=\max\{0,\lambda_{\max}(K_j)\},\qquad
+ \alpha_j^-=\max\{0,-\lambda_{\min}(K_j)\},
+\]
+with both extrema defined to be zero for an empty matrix. For every
+$u\in\Kk$ satisfying $Fu=u$,
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:two-sided-certificate}
+ -(E+\alpha_j^-)\norm u_{\Hh}^2
+ \le p_{>M}[u]\le(E+\alpha_j^+)\norm u_{\Hh}^2.
+\end{equation}
+Singular $G_j$ are allowed. The two sequences $\alpha_j^\pm$ are
+nonincreasing, and
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:constraint-limit}
+ Q_jS_\phi Q_j\longrightarrow P_\Kk S_\phi P_\Kk
+ \quad\hbox{in operator norm as }j\to\infty.
+\end{equation}
+No effective rate of this convergence is asserted.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+The identity for $P_j$ follows from the positive definiteness of the
+true radical Gram, using the linear independence in
+Lemma~\ref{a:lem:radical}. It proves~\eqref{vfourB:constraint-gram}.
+The polar decomposition $Q_jZ=V_jG_j^{1/2}$ gives
+\[
+ Q_jS_\phi Q_j=V_jK_jV_j^*.
+\]
+The partial isometry $V_j$ is isometric on the support of $G_j$, and
+$K_j$ vanishes on its kernel. Thus the two operators have the same
+nonzero eigenvalues, with multiplicities, and their quadratic forms are
+bounded between $-\alpha_j^-I$ and $\alpha_j^+I$ after adjoining the
+possible zero subspaces. For $u\in\Kk$, $Q_ju=u$; for $Fu=u$,
+\eqref{vfourB:filtered-error} bounds the difference between
+$p_{>M}[u]$ and $\ip{S_\phi u}{u}_{\Hh}$ by $E\norm u_{\Hh}^2$.
+This proves~\eqref{vfourB:two-sided-certificate}.
+
+The quantities $\alpha_j^\pm$ are the nonnegative Rayleigh extrema of
+$\pm S_\phi$ on the nested subspaces $\Ran Q_j$, so they cannot increase
+with $j$. The projections $P_j$ increase strongly to $P_\Nn$.
+Since $S_\phi$ has finite rank, strong convergence of these bounded
+projections implies the norm convergence in
+\eqref{vfourB:constraint-limit}.
+\end{proof}
+
+These are safe \emph{absolute} upper bounds for both tail signs, not a
+certified sign. A numerical certificate must additionally enclose the
+actual filtered columns, the weighted Gram entries, the radical inverse,
+and the finite matrix extrema, and must add all operator errors to $E$.
+No such numerical evaluation is supplied here.
+
+\subsection{A resolvent implementation of the filter}
+\label{vfourB:resolvent-filter}
+
+The sharp projection $P_J$ is needed only to prove the error estimate.
+The filter can instead be approximated from actual resolvents. Suppose
+$\phi$ is Lipschitz and supported in $[-R,R]$. For $s>d$, set
+\[
+ R_s=(L_c+s)^{-1},\qquad a=(s-d)^{-1},\qquad
+ h(r)=\begin{cases}\phi(1/r-s),&r>0,\\0,&r=0.\end{cases}
+\]
+On $[0,a]$, the function $h$ is Lipschitz with constant at most
+$\operatorname{Lip}(\phi)(s+R)^2$. Indeed it vanishes for
+$r<1/(s+R)$, and on the remaining interval the reciprocal map has
+Lipschitz constant at most $(s+R)^2$; continuity handles the joining
+point. Its degree-$q$ Bernstein polynomial, scaled to $[0,a]$, is
+\[
+ p_q(r)=\sum_{k=0}^q h(ak/q)\binom qk(r/a)^k(1-r/a)^{q-k}.
+\]
+For $q\ge1$, the binomial variance bound and the spectral theorem imply
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:bernstein-error}
+ \norm{p_q(R_s)-F}
+ \le\frac{\operatorname{Lip}(\phi)(s+R)^2}
+                 {2(s-d)\sqrt q}.
+\end{equation}
+To see this, at $r=a\theta$ write $p_q(r)=\mathbb E h(aK/q)$ with
+$K$ binomial of parameters $q,\theta$. The Lipschitz error is at most
+$a\operatorname{Lip}(h)\sqrt{\theta(1-\theta)/q}$, uniformly bounded
+by the right-hand side. Since $h(0)=0$, $p_q$ has zero constant term.
+
+Actual resolvent columns still require verification. If
+$v\in\dom L_c$ and $f\in\Hh_c$, then
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:resolvent-residual}
+ \norm{v-R_sf}_{\Hh}
+ \le\frac{\norm{(L_c+s)v-f}_{\Hh}}{s-d}.
+\end{equation}
+If a bounded approximation $\widetilde F$ satisfies
+$\norm{\widetilde F-F}\le\eta$, the self-adjoint replacement
+$\widetilde F S\widetilde F^*$, where $S=(A+A^*)/2$, obeys
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:filter-replacement}
+ \norm{\widetilde F S\widetilde F^*-FSF}
+ \le\eta(2+\eta)\norm S
+ \le\eta(2+\eta)\norm A.
+\end{equation}
+Indeed expand the difference into two terms and use
+$\norm F\le1$, $\norm{\widetilde F}\le1+\eta$.
+For a self-adjoint $\widetilde F$, such as the exact real polynomial
+$p_q(R_s)$, the adjoint on the right factor is redundant. These estimates
+specify what must be certified; they do not supply computed resolvent or
+filter columns.
+
+\subsection{Kernel-free collars and the remaining zero-fibre distinction}
+\label{vfourB:collars}
+
+\begin{proposition}[Two different collar limits]
+\label{vfourB:collar-limits}
+Fix $M$ and a bounded Borel $J$ containing a neighborhood of zero. Then
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:kernel-free-limit}
+ E_\delta=\one_{[-\delta,\delta]\setminus\{0\}}(L_c)
+ \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
+ \norm{D_ME_\delta}\longrightarrow0
+ \quad(\delta\downarrow0).
+\end{equation}
+In contrast, for continuous filters $0\le\phi_\delta\le1$ equal to one
+on $[-\delta,\delta]$ and supported in $[-2\delta,2\delta]$,
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourB:known-radical-limit}
+ P_\Kk\phi_\delta(L_c)D_M\phi_\delta(L_c)P_\Kk
+ \longrightarrow (P_0-P_\Nn)D_M(P_0-P_\Nn)
+ \quad\hbox{in operator norm}.
+\end{equation}
+The second limit need not vanish unless additional information about
+the residual zero fibre or its tail compression is available.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+The spectral theorem gives $E_\delta\to0$ strongly, with
+$P_JE_\delta=E_\delta$ for sufficiently small $\delta$.
+Theorem~\ref{vfourB:approximation-theorem} and
+Proposition~\ref{vfourB:rank-schedule} imply that $D_MP_J$ is compact.
+A compact operator multiplied by this strongly null family of
+self-adjoint contractions converges to zero in norm, proving
+\eqref{vfourB:kernel-free-limit}.
+
+Also $F_\delta=\phi_\delta(L_c)\to P_0$ strongly by dominated convergence
+in the spectral theorem, and $F_\delta=P_JF_\delta$ for small $\delta$.
+Since $P_JD_MP_J$ is compact,
+\[
+ F_\delta(P_JD_MP_J)F_\delta\longrightarrow P_0D_MP_0
+ \quad\hbox{in norm}.
+\]
+The known-radical subspace lies in $\ker L_c$, so its projection commutes
+with the functional calculus and $P_\Kk P_0=P_0-P_\Nn$.
+Compressing the last limit by $P_\Kk$ proves
+\eqref{vfourB:known-radical-limit}.
+\end{proof}
+
+The kernel-free conclusion is only an absolute $o(1)$ statement.
+Neither compactness nor this construction supplies $O(\delta)$ or
+$o(\delta)$ control. In particular, choosing a target error
+$\varepsilon=c\delta$ in the explicit schedule does not prove that the
+finite matrix contribution in~\eqref{vfourB:two-sided-certificate} is
+$O(\delta)$. The stronger all-Schatten result of
+Theorem~\ref{vfourD:schatten} addresses negative spectral counting, not
+the missing relative low-collar tail sign. No full-kernel identification,
+whole-space sign conclusion, or proof or disproof of the Riemann
+hypothesis follows from this section.
+
+
+\section{Computable centered ground energy with finite null constraints}
+\label{vfourC:section}
+
+This appendix integrates the conditional enclosure construction of
+\cite{vfourComputable}.  Its object is the bottom of the \emph{actual complete}
+centered form, not the bottom of a fixed prime head or a finite trial matrix.
+Throughout this appendix, as in the main text,
+\[
+ \ip{u}{v}_{\Hh}=\int_{\R}u(x)\overline{v(x)}\,\frac{dx}{W(x)}
+\]
+is linear in its first argument.  The Fourier transform is the ordinary unitary
+transform specified in the common notation.  In particular, matrix entries and
+Fourier features below use this convention, rather than the opposite convention
+in the standalone source.
+
+\subsection{Realization input and the enclosure theorem}
+\label{vfourC:subsec:input}
+
+We use the complete forms \eqref{a:eq:physicalgamma}--\eqref{a:eq:qzero} on
+$X$ of \eqref{a:eq:X}.  Write $L:=B=T_\Gamma-D_b$ for the form-defined weighted
+representative in Section~\ref{sec:weighted-physical}, and $L_c$ for its
+restriction to $\Hh_c$.  The analytic realization inputs are the following:
+$X$ is the closed form domain; the complete prime correction is bounded with
+$\norm{D_b}<10$; $\norm\kappa_{\Hh}=1$ and $L\kappa=-\kappa/2$; and
+\[
+ Cu=u-2m(u)\kappa
+\]
+is the orthogonal projection reducing $L$ to $L_c$.  These are the actual
+form-realization and normalization results of the main text, not consequences
+claimed for an arbitrary computable quadratic form.  No identification with the
+closure of a separately specified minimal symmetric operator is needed here.
+Lemma~\ref{a:lem:radical} supplies independent vectors
+\[
+ r_j=\kappa^{(2j)}-4^{-j}\kappa\in X_c,
+ \qquad q_0(r_j,z)=0\quad(z\in X).
+\]
+Consequently $r_j\in\ker L_c$.  Only
+$\Nn:=\overline{\operatorname{span}\{r_j:j\ge1\}}^{\Hh}
+\subseteq\ker L_c$ is used; equality is not assumed.  If
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:mzero}
+ m_0=\inf\sigma(L_c),
+\end{equation}
+then the correction bound and a nonzero $r_j$ give the safe initial enclosure
+$-16\le m_0\le0$.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Conditional computability of the actual centered energy]
+\label{vfourC:thm:computable}
+Under the stated complete form realization and the effective arithmetic
+estimates specified below, a recursive procedure takes any rational
+$\varepsilon>0$ and terminates with rational endpoints
+\[
+ \ell\le m_0\le u,\qquad u-\ell\le\varepsilon.
+\]
+Any fixed finite independent derivative-radical bank may be imposed exactly,
+including the empty bank.  The procedure requires neither a projection onto the
+entire kernel nor a convergence rate for such finite banks.
+\end{theorem}
+
+The proof is a construction of terminating searches with finite rational
+certificates.  It is not a claim that the complete algorithm has been
+implemented, that a certificate for the actual sign has been evaluated, or that
+a useful running-time bound is known.
+
+\subsection{Effective arithmetic and a rational form core}
+\label{vfourC:subsec:arithmetic}
+
+Here a computable scalar or integral means that arbitrarily narrow rational
+intervals enclosing it can be produced.  All uses of an upper bound in a finite
+certificate mean a rational outward upper enclosure.  The theta series and each
+fixed derivative have effective Gaussian remainders on compact intervals.  For
+$x\ge0$ one may use
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:theta}
+ W(x)\ge\pi^2 e^{4x-\pi e^{2x}},\qquad
+ |\kappa^{(s)}(x)|\le A_s e^{(2s+9/2)x-\pi e^{2x}},
+\end{equation}
+where a rational $A_s$ can be obtained effectively for every fixed $s$.
+Indeed, the first positive theta summand and $\pi>3$ give the lower bound.
+For the upper bound use the polynomial recursion in the proof of
+Lemma~\ref{a:lem:radical}, bound its finitely many coefficients, and sum a finite
+Gaussian head with a geometric bound for its remainder.  Reflection handles
+negative $x$.  These bounds give explicit integrable tails for weighted radical
+norms, moments, and Fourier transforms on compact frequency intervals.  On a
+compact physical interval, a positive lower bound for $W$ is effectively
+certifiable by subdivision and a computable derivative bound.  Thus integration
+against $1/W$ on that interval is effective as well.
+
+The complete Gamma multiplier has the representation
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:gamma}
+ \begin{split}
+ d_\Gamma(t)&=\sum_{h\ge0}\frac{2t^2}{a_h(a_h^2+t^2)},
+       \qquad a_h=2h+\tfrac12,\\
+ 0\le d_\Gamma(t)-\sum_{h<L}\frac{2t^2}{a_h(a_h^2+t^2)}
+   &\le2T^2\left(a_L^{-3}+\frac1{4a_L^2}\right)
+       \qquad(|t|\le T).
+ \end{split}
+\end{equation}
+In addition,
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:gamma-bounds}
+ 0\le d_\Gamma(t)\le17t^2,\qquad
+ \operatorname{Lip}(d_\Gamma|_{[-T,T]})\le34T,\qquad
+ d_\Gamma(t)\ge\tfrac12\log(1+4t^2).
+\end{equation}
+The series follows by expanding the Gamma difference kernel into exponentials;
+the remainder and upper bounds follow by comparison with the sum of $a_h^{-3}$.
+For the lower bound compare the positive decreasing summand, viewed as a
+function of $a_h$, with its integral on $[1/2,\infty)$ with spacing two.
+The corresponding digamma formulas are consistent with
+\cite[Eq.~5.9.16 and Eq.~5.11.2]{DLMF}.  None of these estimates replaces the
+complete Gamma form by a truncated form.
+
+For the complete omitted prime form we may use the already rational bound of
+Lemma~\ref{a:lem:prime}:
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:prime-tail}
+ |p[u]-p_N[u]|\le\bar\tau_N\norm u_{\Hh}^2,\qquad
+ \bar\tau_N=96(N+1)^3(27/512)^{N+1}\longrightarrow0
+ \quad(N\ge2).
+\end{equation}
+This effective cap suffices in place of the sharper exponential cap in
+\cite{vfourComputable}.  Every finite $\Lambda(n)$ is computable by finite
+factorization.  Both orientations and all prime powers remain present in
+\eqref{vfourC:eq:prime-tail}.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Effectively enumerable ambient and centered form cores]
+\label{vfourC:lem:core}
+There is a recursive list of even, compactly supported, $C^1$ piecewise cubic
+splines with rational knots and coefficients in $\mathbb Q+i\mathbb Q$, whose
+values and first derivatives vanish at the support endpoints, which is dense in
+$X$.  Its exact images under $C$ are dense in $X_c$.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+Lemma~\ref{a:lem:Xcore} already gives the compact smooth form core, so only
+rational density and effective enumeration require proof.  On a symmetric
+rational mesh choose rational complex Hermite values and first derivatives at
+the nonnegative knots, impose the even reflection rules, and impose zero value
+and derivative at the outer endpoints.  At zero impose zero first derivative.
+Cubic Hermite interpolation then has rational complex coefficients on each
+interval and is even and $C^1$.  Enumerating the finite rational data enumerates
+all such splines.
+
+For an even compact smooth function, choose a rational support interval slightly
+larger than its support.  Hermite interpolants on increasingly fine symmetric
+rational meshes converge in ordinary $H^1$.  On each fixed mesh the interpolation
+map from its finite endpoint data to $H^1$ is continuous, so rational
+approximations to the values and derivatives preserve convergence; the imposed
+outer zero data agree with the target.  On the common compact support,
+$1/W$ is bounded, while \eqref{vfourC:eq:gamma-bounds} bounds the Gamma form by
+$17\norm{v'}_2^2$.  Hence this approximation converges in $X$.  Finally $m$ is
+$\Hh$-bounded and $\kappa\in X$, so $C$ is bounded on $X$ and maps the dense list
+to a dense list in $X_c$.  This argument needs no computable approximation rate
+for an arbitrary element of $X$.
+\end{proof}
+
+Each listed spline $v$ has computable $q_0[v]$, $\norm v_{\Hh}^2$, and $m(v)$.
+To see the only unbounded-frequency issue explicitly, set $D_v=\norm{v''}_1$;
+two integrations by parts give
+\[
+ |\widehat v(t)|\le\frac{D_v}{\sqrt{2\pi}\,t^2},\qquad
+ \int_{|t|>T}d_\Gamma(t)|\widehat v(t)|^2\,dt
+       \le\frac{17D_v^2}{\pi T}.
+\]
+Here $v''$ is the piecewise ordinary second derivative; the $C^1$ endpoint
+conditions eliminate distributional boundary terms.  Compact quadrature and
+\eqref{vfourC:eq:gamma} compute the retained Gamma part, while
+\eqref{vfourC:eq:prime-tail} pays the entire omitted prime sum.  Exact centering
+uses the identities
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:centering}
+ q_0[Cv]=q_0[v]+2|m(v)|^2,\qquad
+ \norm{Cv}_{\Hh}^2=\norm v_{\Hh}^2-4|m(v)|^2.
+\end{equation}
+The moment is a computable real or complex number, not a rounded value declared
+to vanish exactly.
+
+For rational $b<0$, enumerate the trials and their interval precision until a
+strictly negative rational upper enclosure for
+$q_0[Cv]-b\norm{Cv}_{\Hh}^2$ is obtained.  This is a sound semidecision for
+$m_0<b$.  It is complete by the variational principle, form-core density, and
+continuity of the shifted form.  It need not terminate at $m_0=b$.
+
+\subsection{True finite null Grams and the shifted positive reference}
+\label{vfourC:subsec:reference}
+
+Fix a finite independent bank $r_1,\ldots,r_j$ and define the synthesis map
+$V_jz=\sum_{i=1}^jz_i r_i$.  Its actual weighted Gram and projection are
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:null-gram}
+ H_j=V_j^*V_j
+       =[\ip{r_\ell}{r_i}_{\Hh}]_{i,\ell=1}^j,
+ \qquad P_j=V_jH_j^{-1}V_j^*.
+\end{equation}
+These are not identity-Gram substitutions.  Independence makes $H_j$ positive
+definite, and effective entry refinement eventually certifies this fact and
+computes its inverse, without a prescribed condition number.  Empty-bank
+formulas mean $P_0=0$ and omit the corresponding matrices.  The exact map
+$(I-P_j)C$ is form-continuous and maps the spline list densely into
+$X\cap S$, where
+\[
+ S=\operatorname{span}\{\kappa,r_1,\ldots,r_j\}^{\perp}\subset\Hh_c.
+\]
+If $z=V_j^*Cv$, its trial values can be computed from
+\[
+ q_0[(I-P_j)Cv]=q_0[Cv],\qquad
+ \norm{(I-P_j)Cv}_{\Hh}^2=\norm{Cv}_{\Hh}^2-z^*H_j^{-1}z.
+\]
+Since $b<0$ and the removed bank is exactly null, a strict negative shifted
+trial in $X_c$ implies one in $X\cap S$.  Thus the preceding negative
+semidecision may also enforce the finite bank exactly.
+
+Fix a rational shift $\sigma>0$.  Because $L_cP_j=0$,
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:null-decomposition}
+ L_c+\sigma I
+   =\sigma P_j+(I-P_j)(L_c+\sigma I)(I-P_j)
+\end{equation}
+as an operator decomposition, or equivalently as a form decomposition.  For
+$N\ge2$ such that $\bar\tau_N<\sigma$, put
+\[
+ \delta'=\sigma-\bar\tau_N>0,\qquad
+ c_N(t)=c_\Gamma+2\sum_{2\le n\le N}\frac{\Lambda(n)}{\sqrt n}
+                                  \cos(t\log n),
+\]
+\[
+ a_N=(d_\Gamma-c_N)_+,\qquad b_N=(c_N-d_\Gamma)_+,\qquad
+ C_N=|c_\Gamma|+2\sum_{2\le n\le N}\frac{\Lambda(n)}{\sqrt n}.
+\]
+Here $x_+=\max(x,0)$ is computable without deciding the sign of $x$.
+Let $A_{\mathrm{ref}}$ represent the ambient positive closed form
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:reference-form}
+ p_{\mathrm{ref}}[u]=\int_{\R}a_N(t)|\widehat u(t)|^2\,dt
+                         +\delta'\norm u_{\Hh}^2,
+\end{equation}
+and let $A_S$ be its form restriction to $S$.  Since
+$|a_N-d_\Gamma|\le C_N$, these forms have domains $X$ and $X\cap S$,
+respectively, and $A_S\ge\delta'I$.  The complete tail bound gives
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:head-comparison}
+ q_0[u]+\sigma\norm u_{\Hh}^2
+ \ge p_{\mathrm{ref}}[u]-\int_{\R}b_N(t)|\widehat u(t)|^2\,dt
+ \quad(u\in X\cap S).
+\end{equation}
+Choose a rational $\bar C>C_N$ and an integer $T$ for which
+$\tfrac12\log(1+4T^2)>\bar C$ is certified.  Such a search terminates, and
+\eqref{vfourC:eq:gamma-bounds} implies $b_N=0$ for $|t|\ge T$.
+No equality test at a zero of $d_\Gamma-c_N$ occurs.
+
+Write $B_S$ for the nonnegative bounded operator on $S$ with form
+$\int b_N|\widehat u|^2$, and set
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:BS}
+ K=A_S^{-1/2}B_SA_S^{-1/2}.
+\end{equation}
+For later computation let $V=(\kappa,r_1,\ldots,r_j)$ denote the finite synthesis
+map, $R=A_{\mathrm{ref}}^{-1}$, and $P_S$ the weighted orthogonal projection onto
+$S$.  The inverse restricted by these \emph{exact} finite constraints is
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:restricted-inverse}
+ R_S=R-RV(V^*RV)^{-1}V^*R=A_S^{-1}P_S,
+\end{equation}
+where the last operator is extended by zero on $S^\perp$.
+Indeed, applied to an input $f\in\Hh$, the first expression belongs to $S$,
+and its reference-form pairing with every $z\in X\cap S$ is
+$\ip{f}{z}_{\Hh}$.
+Uniqueness of the positive restricted-form solution proves the formula.
+The finite inverse Gram $V^*RV$ is positive definite because $R$ is positive
+and injective and the columns of $V$ are independent.  Its entries, like those
+of $H_j$, retain the actual weighted inner product.
+
+\subsection{A compact positive Birman--Schwinger gate}
+\label{vfourC:subsec:matrix}
+
+For $0\le t\le T$ define
+\[
+ g_t(x)=\frac{W(x)\cos(tx)}{\sqrt{2\pi}},\qquad
+ h_t=A_S^{-1/2}P_Sg_t.
+\]
+For even $u$, $\widehat u(t)=\ip{u}{g_t}_{\Hh}$.  With the convention
+$(hh^*)u=\ip{u}{h}_{\Hh}h$, one therefore has
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:feature}
+ K=2\int_0^T b_N(t)h_th_t^*\,dt.
+\end{equation}
+The normalization $\int c^2W=1/4$ implies
+$\int W\le1/4$ and $\int x^2W\le1/2$; for the second inequality use
+$2y^2\le\cosh^2y$ at $y=x/2$.  Hence, with
+$G_0=(8\pi)^{-1/2}$ and $G_1=(4\pi)^{-1/2}$,
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:feature-bounds}
+ \norm{h_t}_{\Hh}\le\frac{G_0}{\sqrt{\delta'}},\qquad
+ \norm{h_t-h_s}_{\Hh}\le\frac{G_1}{\sqrt{\delta'}}|t-s|.
+\end{equation}
+In particular the integral in \eqref{vfourC:eq:feature} converges in trace norm,
+so $K$ is compact and positive.  Compactness, not just boundedness, will be
+essential in the completeness argument.
+
+Let
+\[
+ L_N=2\sum_{2\le n\le N}\Lambda(n)n^{-1/2}\log n,
+ \qquad L_b=34T+L_N.
+\]
+Then $0\le b_N\le C_N$ and $\operatorname{Lip}b_N\le L_b$.  For $r$ equal
+midpoint cells, $\Delta=T/r$ and $t_i=(i+1/2)\Delta$, define
+\begin{align}
+ K_r&=\sum_{i=0}^{r-1}w_i h_{t_i}h_{t_i}^*,
+       \qquad w_i=2b_N(t_i)\Delta,\label{vfourC:eq:finite-K}\\
+ M_r&=\operatorname{diag}(\sqrt{w_i})
+     [\ip{R_Sg_{t_\ell}}{g_{t_i}}_{\Hh}]_{i,\ell=0}^{r-1}
+       \operatorname{diag}(\sqrt{w_i}).\label{vfourC:eq:finite-M}
+\end{align}
+The nonzero spectra of $M_r$ and $K_r$ coincide.  All displayed features and
+operators preserve real functions, so $M_r$ is real symmetric.  Zero weights
+cause no problem: the nonnegative square-root map is computable at zero and
+no division by a weight is used.  The rank-one Lipschitz estimate and midpoint
+integration give
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:quadrature}
+ \norm{K-K_r}\le\eta_{\mathrm{quad}}
+ :=\frac{T\Delta}{2\delta'}
+       (L_bG_0^2+2C_NG_0G_1).
+\end{equation}
+For verification replace the constants on the right by rational upper bounds.
+The estimate follows by integrating $|t-t_i|$ over each cell and using
+$\norm{hh^*-kk^*}\le(\norm h+\norm k)\norm{h-k}$.
+
+Let $\widetilde M$ be a symmetric rational approximation to $M_r$, with a
+certified rational bound
+$\norm{M_r-\widetilde M}\le\eta_{\mathrm{entries}}$ obtained, for example,
+from maximum absolute row sums.  The finite gate is
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:gate}
+ I-\widetilde M
+       -(\eta_{\mathrm{entries}}+\eta_{\mathrm{quad}})I>0.
+\end{equation}
+It is decidable for a given rational matrix by exact Sylvester or
+$LDL^{\mathsf T}$ tests.  A successful strict test also supplies a rational
+$0<\gamma\le1$ for which $I-K\ge\gamma I$: refine a rational lower margin for
+the tested matrix and use the spectral correspondence, allowing for the zero
+spectrum of $K_r$.  Consequently \eqref{vfourC:eq:head-comparison} yields
+\[
+ q_0[u]+\sigma\norm u_{\Hh}^2
+        \ge\gamma\delta'\norm u_{\Hh}^2\qquad(u\in X\cap S).
+\]
+Equation~\eqref{vfourC:eq:null-decomposition} restores the removed radical bank
+with margin $\min\{\gamma\delta',\sigma\}>0$ on all of $X_c$.
+
+\subsection{Computable inverse upper bounds and primal lower bounds}
+\label{vfourC:subsec:inverse}
+
+We now compute the entries in \eqref{vfourC:eq:restricted-inverse} and
+\eqref{vfourC:eq:finite-M}; an inverse oracle is not an assumption.  Let
+$\mathcal F_2f=\widehat f$ denote the ordinary unitary Fourier transform, let
+$J:\Hh\to\Le$ be inclusion, and fix a rational
+$\bar W\ge\norm W_\infty$, for instance $\bar W=2$ using
+Lemma~\ref{a:lem:explicitW}.  For integers $k\ge1$ put
+\[
+ m_k(t)=\min(a_N(t),k)\one_{[-k,k]}(t),\quad
+ S_k=J^*\mathcal F_2^{-1}m_k\mathcal F_2J,\quad A_k=\delta'I+S_k.
+\]
+The nonnegative forms increase to $p_{\mathrm{ref}}$, so monotone convergence
+of closed forms gives $A_k^{-1}\downarrow R$ strongly.  Since
+$0\le S_k\le k\bar W I$, the rational constants and operators
+\[
+ \alpha_k=\delta'+k\bar W,\qquad
+ Y_k=I-A_k/\alpha_k,\qquad q_k=1-\delta'/\alpha_k
+\]
+satisfy $0\le Y_k\le q_k I$ with $q_k<1$.  Thus, for $\ell\ge0$,
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:inverse-upper}
+ P_{k,\ell}=\alpha_k^{-1}\sum_{s=0}^{\ell}Y_k^s,
+ \qquad
+ R\le A_k^{-1}\le P_{k,\ell}
+                  +q_k^{\ell+1}\delta'^{-1}I.
+\end{equation}
+The polynomial $P_{k,\ell}$ by itself is \emph{not} a claimed lower bound for
+$R$.
+
+For each finite input vector $f$ needed here, the upper quadratic endpoint in
+\eqref{vfourC:eq:inverse-upper} is computable.  Its degree-zero term uses
+$\norm f_{\Hh}^2$, and for $n\ge1$ the other terms reduce to compact cube
+integrals
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:cube}
+ \begin{split}
+ \ip{S_k^nf}{f}_{\Hh}
+ ={}&\int_{[-k,k]^n}\overline{\widehat f(t_1)}\widehat f(t_n)
+       \prod_{h=1}^{n}m_k(t_h)\\
+ &\hspace{13mm}\cdot
+       \prod_{h=1}^{n-1}\frac{\widehat W(t_h-t_{h+1})}{\sqrt{2\pi}}
+       \,dt_1\cdots dt_n.
+ \end{split}
+\end{equation}
+The family of inputs is $g_{t_i},\kappa,r_1,\ldots,r_j$ and their finite
+polarization combinations.  Equation~\eqref{vfourC:eq:theta} gives computable
+transforms, moments, weighted norms, and compact-frequency moduli of continuity
+for these vectors and for $W$.  On the integration cube,
+$\min(a_N,k)$ is computably Lipschitz.  The sharp frequency cutoff may jump at
+$\pm k$, so no global Lipschitz assertion about $m_k$ is required: those
+endpoints are the boundary of the fixed integration cube.  Subdivision with
+these moduli gives certified rational enclosures for \eqref{vfourC:eq:cube}.
+The formula uses the full Fourier representation; evenness of $W$ makes the
+convolution kernel real and even, consistently with the chosen inner-product
+convention.
+
+Independent lower endpoints follow from the variational identity
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:inverse-lower}
+ \begin{split}
+ 2\operatorname{Re}\ip{v}{f}_{\Hh}-p_{\mathrm{ref}}[v]
+       &\le\ip{Rf}{f}_{\Hh},\\
+ \sup_{v\text{ in the rational spline list}}
+       \bigl(2\operatorname{Re}\ip{v}{f}_{\Hh}
+                      -p_{\mathrm{ref}}[v]\bigr)
+       &=\ip{Rf}{f}_{\Hh}.
+ \end{split}
+\end{equation}
+The full form is used.  Indeed the gap for an arbitrary $v\in X$ is
+$p_{\mathrm{ref}}[v-Rf]$, and the form core approximates $Rf\in X$.
+All trial expressions are computable.  In particular, for a spline $v$,
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourC:eq:reference-tail}
+ \int_{|t|>T}a_N(t)|\widehat v(t)|^2\,dt
+       \le\frac{D_v^2}{\pi}
+                   \left(\frac{17}{T}+\frac{C_N}{3T^3}\right).
+\end{equation}
+
+Dovetail $k,\ell$, the spline trials, and all integration precisions.  Retain the
+smallest certified rational upper endpoint from
+\eqref{vfourC:eq:inverse-upper} and the largest certified rational lower endpoint
+from \eqref{vfourC:eq:inverse-lower}.  For every rational tolerance $\zeta>0$,
+strong convergence of $A_k^{-1}$, geometric convergence in $\ell$, and core
+density force the rational endpoint gap below $\zeta$ at a finite stage.
+The stopping condition is the observable rational comparison
+$U_f-L_f<\zeta$, not an unobservable limit or a supplied convergence rate.
+Polarization computes all required mixed entries.  Refine the positive matrix
+$V^*RV$ until a positive rational lower eigenvalue is certified, compute its
+inverse with controlled error, and apply \eqref{vfourC:eq:restricted-inverse}.
+This proves termination of every finite inverse-entry job and hence of every
+requested matrix-entry enclosure.  No useful bound on their cost follows.
+
+\subsection{Completeness for uniform shifted coercivity}
+\label{vfourC:subsec:coercivity}
+
+\begin{proposition}[Sound and complete strict-shift semidecision]
+\label{vfourC:prop:strict-shift}
+For rational $\sigma>0$, fair enumeration of the finite gates
+\eqref{vfourC:eq:gate} is a sound semidecision for
+\[
+ q_0[u]+\sigma\norm u_{\Hh}^2\ge c\norm u_{\Hh}^2
+       \quad(u\in X_c)\quad\text{for some }c>0,
+\]
+equivalently $m_0+\sigma>0$.  It is complete for this \emph{uniform} coercivity
+condition and need not halt when $m_0+\sigma=0$.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Soundness is the conclusion of the finite gate and the exact radical
+decomposition.  For completeness suppose the displayed margin $c>0$ exists,
+without requiring that it be known.  Choose $N$ large enough that
+$\bar\tau_N<\sigma$ and $2\bar\tau_N<c$.  On $X\cap S$ the reference difference
+has the exact expression
+\[
+ p_{\mathrm{ref}}[u]-\ip{B_Su}{u}_{\Hh}
+   =q_0[u]+\sigma\norm u_{\Hh}^2
+       +(p-p_N)[u]-\bar\tau_N\norm u_{\Hh}^2,
+\]
+and is therefore at least $(c-2\bar\tau_N)\norm u_{\Hh}^2$.
+If the compact positive operator $K$ had $\norm K\ge1$, it would have a
+nonzero eigenvector $y$ at an eigenvalue $\lambda\ge1$.  The nonzero vector
+$u=A_S^{-1/2}y\in X\cap S$ would satisfy
+\[
+ p_{\mathrm{ref}}[u]-\ip{B_Su}{u}_{\Hh}
+      =\norm y_{\Hh}^2-\ip{Ky}{y}_{\Hh}
+      =(1-\lambda)\norm y_{\Hh}^2\le0,
+\]
+contradicting the uniform lower bound.  Thus $\norm K<1$.  Increasing midpoint
+resolution drives \eqref{vfourC:eq:quadrature} to zero; the terminating inverse
+procedures drive every finite matrix-entry error to zero.  Consequently some
+finite rational gate succeeds.  Fair dovetailing over eligible $N$, midpoint
+resolutions, and finite precision requests reaches it.  Each request involves
+only finitely many terminating scalar and inverse jobs, so the search does not
+wait forever on one unsatisfied proposed gate.
+\end{proof}
+
+Pointwise strict positivity without a uniform weighted-$\Hh$ margin is not the
+hypothesis of this completeness statement.  In particular the proof does not
+silently replace strict shifted coercivity by nonnegativity at an endpoint.
+
+\subsection{Equality-safe rational brackets and finite obligations}
+\label{vfourC:subsec:brackets}
+
+\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{vfourC:thm:computable}]
+Start with rational endpoints $[\ell,u]=[-16,0]$.  If $u-\ell>\varepsilon$, set
+\[
+ a=\ell+(u-\ell)/3,\qquad b=\ell+2(u-\ell)/3.
+\]
+Then $a<b<0$.  Dovetail the positive gate for the rational shift $\sigma=-a$
+and the strict-trial search for $m_0<b$.  If the gate succeeds, replace
+$\ell$ by $a$; if the trial succeeds first, replace $u$ by $b$.
+Both updates preserve the enclosure.  At least one search terminates:
+if $m_0\ge b$, then $m_0-a>0$, so the gate terminates; if $m_0\le a$, then
+$m_0<b$, so the trial search terminates; and in the overlap both conditions
+hold.  In particular equality at either tested threshold cannot block both
+branches.  Each update reduces the width by a factor $2/3$.  Thus for
+$0<\varepsilon<16$ at most
+\[
+ \left\lceil\frac{\log(16/\varepsilon)}{\log(3/2)}\right\rceil
+\]
+outer updates are needed.  This is only a bound on the number of outer updates,
+not a bound on the duration of their inner searches.  For $\varepsilon\ge16$
+the initial interval already suffices.
+\end{proof}
+
+A finite positive certificate must retain all of the following:
+\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
+ \item the prime cutoff, the complete rational tail cap, the positive rational
+       shift $\delta'$, and a certified compact support bound for $b_N$;
+ \item the theta and Gamma truncation errors, physical and Fourier tail bounds,
+       and rational quadrature enclosures used in all entries;
+ \item the actual finite null Gram when the bank is used, the positive inverse
+       Gram $V^*RV$, its certified inverse error, and the upper/primal-lower
+       endpoint evidence for each required inverse entry;
+ \item the rational matrix $\widetilde M$, complete scalar and entry error
+       debit, midpoint error debit, and an exact positive-definiteness witness
+       for \eqref{vfourC:eq:gate}.
+\end{enumerate}
+A finite negative certificate consists of the rational spline data, the exact
+centering identities (and true finite-bank correction, if imposed), and a
+strict rational upper enclosure for the complete shifted trial form.
+Verification checks finite evidence; it does not invoke an exact-zero test,
+an exact sign oracle, or a computed projection onto the entire kernel.
+
+The conclusion is conditional on the specified actual form realization and
+effective arithmetic, as in \cite{vfourComputable}.  It establishes
+computability as a real number, not decidability of $m_0=0$.  If $m_0<0$, the
+strict negative search eventually supplies a witness; if $m_0=0$, arbitrarily
+small absolute brackets alone do not certify equality.  Neither the actual
+sign, a practical complexity estimate, an effective infinite-radical repair
+rate, nor a proof or disproof of the Riemann hypothesis is supplied.
+
+
+\section{Positive-source compression and prior spectral results}\label{vfourD:section}
+This appendix incorporates the source-bank argument of \cite{vfourSourceCompression} as an alternative proof for the same qualitative spectral conclusions previously obtained in \cite{vfourSchubr}. The earlier result and its relationship to the current realization are stated in Section~\ref{vfourD:prior}. No historical priority or better-constant claim is made. Unlike the convention used in the main text, inner products in this appendix are conjugate-linear in the first argument.
+
+We retain the actual form $q_0$, $\Hh$, $X$, $m$ and weight $W$ from Section~\ref{a:sec:energy}. Let $\Hh_c=\ker m$, $X_c=X\cap\Hh_c$, $C u=u-2m(u)\kappa$, and let $L_c$ be the form representative of $q_0$ on $X_c$. All identities used below are on this complete centered form domain. The full theta normalization and envelope give
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourD:Wbound}
+ \norm\kappa_{\Hh}=1,\qquad B_0:=\int_\R W(x)\,dx\le\tfrac14,\qquad
+ 0<W(x)\le40e^{4|x|-\pi e^{2|x|}}\le40e^{4|x|-3e^{2|x|}}.
+\end{equation}
+The Gamma symbol $d_\Gamma$ and $c_\Gamma$ are those of the complete form; $d_\Gamma(t)\ge\frac12\log(1+4t^2)$, and every prime power and both shift orientations are retained.
+
+For integer $N\ge1$, write
+\begin{gather*}
+ c_N(t)=c_\Gamma+2\sum_{2\le n\le N}\frac{\Lambda(n)}{\sqrt n}\cos(t\log n),\quad
+ C_N=c_\Gamma+2\sum_{2\le n\le N}\frac{\Lambda(n)}{\sqrt n},\\
+ a_N=(d_\Gamma-c_N)_+,\quad b_N=(c_N-d_\Gamma)_+,\quad
+ T_N=\tfrac12\sqrt{e^{2C_N}-1}>0.
+\end{gather*}
+The scalar $b_N$ is nonnegative, even, vanishes for $|t|\ge T_N$, and satisfies
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourD:b-integral}
+ \int_0^{T_N}b_N(t)dt\le T_N-\tfrac12\arctan(2T_N)\le T_N.
+\end{equation}
+For $g_t(x)=W(x)\cos(tx)/\sqrt{2\pi}$, the actual positive trace-class deficit on $\Hh_c$ is
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourD:Bform}
+ B_N=2\int_0^{T_N}b_N(t)(Cg_t)(Cg_t)^*dt,\qquad
+ q_0[u]=\int a_N(t)|\widehat u(t)|^2dt-\ip u{B_Nu}-\ip u{D_{>N}u}
+\end{equation}
+for every $u\in X_c$. The complete centered tail is bounded self-adjoint and
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourD:tail}
+ \norm{D_{>N}}\le\tau_N\le160(N+1)^2e^{-\pi(N+1)}.
+\end{equation}
+The full-domain form identity follows from finite-head Plancherel and the entire prime-tail norm convergence; the last two terms retain their actual signs. In particular,
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourD:lower}
+ q_0[u]\ge-\ip u{B_Nu}-\tau_N\norm u_{\Hh}^2\qquad(u\in X_c).
+\end{equation}
+For $\delta>0$ define the \emph{closed} negative count
+\[N_-(\delta)=\dim\operatorname{Ran}\mathbf1_{(-\infty,-\delta]}(L_c),\]
+counting eigenvalues at $-\delta$ and multiplicities. A publicly posted preceding trace paper \cite{vfourTrace}, which uses the same actual input realization and classical trace-count principle, proves $N_-(\delta)\le\delta^{-1}e^{O(\sqrt{\log(1/\delta)})}$ and $(L_c)_-\in\mathfrak S_p$ for $p>1$. Earlier, Schubr \cite[Theorem~1.1]{vfourSchubr} obtained a subpolynomial negative-count estimate and all positive Schatten exponents for a constrained weighted Weil operator. Section~\ref{vfourD:prior} explains the even-sector form identification. The argument below is an alternative proof with an explicit constant, not a claim of first discovery or of a better constant.
+
+\subsection{Relation to earlier work}\label{vfourD:prior}
+Schubr's publicly posted edition~3 \cite{vfourSchubr}, Theorem~1.1, gives a subpolynomial negative spectral count and membership of the negative part in every $\mathfrak S_p$, $p>0$, for the complete constrained operator $H$ defined there. We record the connection with the present form, rather than claiming those consequences as first results here.
+
+In \cite[Section~2, equations (4)--(6)]{vfourSchubr}, the source $\psi$ is our $\kappa$, the weight $w=\psi\cosh(x/2)$, $s^2=\psi/\cosh(x/2)=2W$, the multiplier $a$ equals $d_\Gamma$, and the scalar $m_0$ there is $-c_\Gamma$. The unitary map $U_W:\Hh\to L^2_{\rm even}(dx)$ is $U_Wu=u/\sqrt W$. For even $u$, the second constraint of \cite{vfourSchubr} is automatic by parity, while its first is $\langle\sqrt w,U_Wu\rangle=\sqrt2\,m(u)$. Thus $U_W\Hh_c$ is its even constrained space. On centered even compact-smooth functions, the full form of \cite{vfourSchubr} at $U_Wu$ is exactly $2q_0[u]$: $sU_Wu=\sqrt2u$, both complete prime orientations are retained, and its nonunitary Fourier transform with $dt/(2\pi)$ matches our unitary convention.
+
+The form closures agree under the stated source inputs. Multiplication by $\sqrt W$ or its reciprocal maps compact smooth even functions bijectively to compact smooth even functions because $W$ is smooth and strictly positive. The ambient compact-smooth form core in Lemma~\ref{a:lem:Xcore} yields a centered compact-smooth core: after approximating $u\in X_c$, subtract from each approximant a scalar multiple of one fixed even compact-smooth test with nonzero $m$; the scalar tends to zero by continuity of $m$. This is precisely the transformed even constrained core in \cite{vfourSchubr}. Consequently the even restriction of its form-defined operator is unitarily equivalent to $2L_c$. The full constrained operator there reduces under parity, so its negative-count and all-Schatten estimates imply the corresponding conclusions for $L_c$; the use of strict thresholds there versus the closed threshold here does not alter the asymptotic, since the closed $L_c$ threshold $-\delta$ is contained in its strict range below $-\delta/2$, corresponding to the strict $H$ threshold $-\delta$. The explicit numerical constant $40$ below is one admissible bound, not a claim to improve the constants of \cite{vfourSchubr}.
+
+\subsection{A centered source bank and its true Gram}\label{vfourD:bank}
+Fix $R>0$ and integer $m\ge0$. In $\Hh_c$ set
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourD:bankdef}
+ v_j=\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi}}C\!\left(W(x)x^{2j}\mathbf1_{[-R,R]}(x)\right),\quad 0\le j\le m,\quad V=(v_0,\ldots,v_m),\quad r=m+1.
+\end{equation}
+Each vector belongs to $\Hh_c$, and the bank is independent. Indeed, if $C(Wp\mathbf1_{[-R,R]})=0$ for an even polynomial $p$, the uncentered vector is a scalar multiple of $\kappa$. Outside the interval it vanishes while $\kappa>0$, so the scalar is zero; then $W>0$ on the interval forces $p=0$. This proof applies to complex coefficients.
+
+Let
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourD:gram}
+ a_j=\int_{-R}^{R}\kappa(x)x^{2j}dx,\qquad w_\ell=\int_{-R}^{R}W(x)x^{2\ell}dx,\qquad
+ G_{ij}=\ip{v_i}{v_j}=\frac{w_{i+j}-a_i a_j}{2\pi}.
+\end{equation}
+Thus $G=V^*V$ is positive definite as a matrix. The integrals in \eqref{vfourD:gram} are over the indicated \emph{finite} interval, and the centering terms are retained. Define
+\[P=VG^{-1}V^*,\qquad Q=I-P\quad\text{on }\Hh_c.\]
+This is the true weighted orthogonal projection, not a coefficient-identity Gram or a projection onto the physical kernel. The source bank is required only to lie in $\Hh_c$. We do not require or use $v_j\in X_c$, $v_j\in\dom L_c$, or preservation of either domain by $P$ or $Q$.
+
+\subsection{Trace defect and an explicit rank}\label{vfourD:compression}
+\begin{theorem}\label{vfourD:defect}
+For every $\varepsilon>0$, with $T=T_N$, define
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourD:parameters}
+ h=\max\{1,T/\varepsilon\},\quad R=\tfrac12\log\log(80h),\quad
+ r=\left\lceil\max\left\{1,3TR,\tfrac12\log_2(2h)\right\}\right\rceil,\quad m=r-1.
+\end{equation}
+For the bank \eqref{vfourD:bankdef} and $Q$ above,
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourD:traceerror}
+ \operatorname{tr}(QB_NQ)<\varepsilon,\qquad
+ r\le3TR+\tfrac12\log_2(2h)+2.
+\end{equation}
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+Set $k=2m+2=2r$ and $p_{m,t}(x)=\sum_{j=0}^m(-1)^j(tx)^{2j}/(2j)!$. For $|x|\le R$ and $0\le t\le T$, Taylor's theorem to order $2m+1$ gives
+\[|\cos(tx)-p_{m,t}(x)|\le E:=\frac{(TR)^k}{k!}.
+\]
+The centered truncated Taylor vector belongs to $\operatorname{Ran}P$. Since $C$ is contractive, the projection distance, with disjoint inside and outside regions, satisfies
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourD:distance}
+ \norm{QCg_t}_{\Hh}^2\le\frac{W_{\rm tail}(R)+B_0E^2}{2\pi},\qquad W_{\rm tail}(R)=\int_{|x|>R}W(x)dx.
+\end{equation}
+Consequently, by \eqref{vfourD:Bform} and \eqref{vfourD:b-integral},
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourD:trace-majorant}
+ \operatorname{tr}(QB_NQ)=2\int_0^T b_N(t)\norm{QCg_t}_{\Hh}^2dt
+ \le\frac{T}{\pi}\left(W_{\rm tail}(R)+B_0E^2\right).
+\end{equation}
+This bound includes the full centered tail of each source: no uncentered truncation is treated as already centered.
+
+For $Y=e^{2R}\ge1$, the envelope in \eqref{vfourD:Wbound} has logarithmic derivative at most $-2$ on $x\ge0$. Integrating the tangent exponential majorant on both half-lines yields
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourD:Wtail}
+ W_{\rm tail}(R)\le40Y^2e^{-3Y}\le40e^{-Y}.
+\end{equation}
+With \eqref{vfourD:parameters}, $Y=\log(80h)$, so $W_{\rm tail}(R)\le1/(2h)$. The factorial estimate $k!\ge(k/e)^k$, $e<3$, and $k=2r\ge6TR$ give $E\le2^{-k}\le1/(2h)$. Using $B_0\le1/4$ in \eqref{vfourD:trace-majorant},
+\[\operatorname{tr}(QB_NQ)\le\frac{T}{\pi}\left(\frac1{2h}+\frac1{16h^2}\right)
+ \le\frac{9T}{16\pi h}\le\frac{9\varepsilon}{16\pi}<\varepsilon.
+\]
+The rank bound follows from $\lceil\max(a,b,c)\rceil\le a+b+c+2$ for the displayed nonnegative quantities.
+\end{proof}
+For a certified finite implementation, use rigorous upper bounds for $T,h,R$ and choose an even $k$ satisfying the displayed inequalities strictly; if the radius is enlarged, recheck $k\ge6TR$. This avoids deciding whether a computable real is exactly an integer. It does not provide a bound on the condition number of $G$, required arithmetic precision, or total runtime.
+
+\subsection{Negative count and Schatten classes}\label{vfourD:count}
+\begin{theorem}\label{vfourD:main}
+For $0<\delta\le1$, the actual centered negative spectral count satisfies
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourD:countbound}
+ N_-(\delta)<\exp\!\left(40\sqrt{1+\log(1/\delta)}\right).
+\end{equation}
+In particular $N_-(\delta)\le\exp(O(\sqrt{1+\log(1/\delta)}))$ as $\delta\downarrow0$. The constant $40$ is deliberately loose.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+Put
+\begin{equation}\label{vfourD:Ndelta}
+ M=\max\{8,\lceil\log_2(2/\delta)\rceil\},\qquad N=M-1.
+\end{equation}
+Then $\tau_N\le\delta/2$. Indeed, for $M\ge8$,
+\[160M^2e^{-\pi M}\le e^{-\pi M/2}\le2^{-M}\le\delta/2.
+\]
+The first inequality holds at $M=8$ because $160\cdot8^2<(5/2)^{12}<e^{3\cdot8/2}<e^{\pi8/2}$, and its ratio improves for $M\ge8$; the second follows from $\pi/2>\log2$.
+
+Use Theorem~\ref{vfourD:defect} for $B_N$ with $\varepsilon=\delta/4$. If the closed negative spectral subspace had dimension greater than $r$, it would contain a nonzero $u\in\operatorname{Ran}Q$. Lower boundedness of $L_c$ places that spectral subspace in $\dom L_c\subset X_c$. Since $B_N\ge0$,
+\[\ip u{B_Nu}=\ip u{QB_NQu}\le\operatorname{tr}(QB_NQ)\norm u_{\Hh}^2<\frac\delta4\norm u_{\Hh}^2.
+\]
+Then \eqref{vfourD:lower} gives $q_0[u]>-3\delta\norm u_{\Hh}^2/4$, contradicting $q_0[u]\le-\delta\norm u_{\Hh}^2$. Thus $N_-(\delta)\le r$. This argument uses only that $P$ is a bounded finite-rank projection on $\Hh_c$; it never applies $L_c$ to a bank vector or assumes $P X_c\subset X_c$.
+
+For the displayed constant, the elementary full von Mangoldt estimate in \cite{vfourTrace} gives $C_N\le16+6\sqrt N$. Put $L=1+\log(1/\delta)\ge1$ and $x=\sqrt L$. From \eqref{vfourD:Ndelta} and $\log2>1/2$, $N<8L$; hence $C_N<34x$ and $T<e^{34x}$. With $h=\max\{1,4T/\delta\}$, $\log h<36L$, so
+\[R=\tfrac12\log\log(80h)<2+\tfrac12\log L\le\tfrac52x<e^{2x}.
+\]
+Also $\tfrac12\log_2(2h)<37L$. The explicit rank formula gives
+\[r<3e^{36x}+37L+2\le42e^{36x}<e^{40x},
+\]
+using $L=x^2\le e^{36x}$ and $e^4>42$. This proves \eqref{vfourD:countbound}. The constants depend only on the displayed actual input estimates, not on a Gram condition number or an inverse computation.
+\end{proof}
+\begin{corollary}\label{vfourD:schatten}
+The negative part $(L_c)_-=(-L_c)_+$ is compact and belongs to $\mathfrak S_p$ for every $p>0$, in particular to $\mathfrak S_1$.
+\end{corollary}
+\begin{proof}
+The count is finite at every positive threshold, and $L_c$ is lower bounded. Its negative part is therefore the norm limit of finite-rank spectral truncations. If $\mu_n$ are its nonzero eigenvalues, the layer-cake formula for $p>0$ gives
+\[\sum_n\mu_n^p=p\int_0^{\norm{(L_c)_-}}\delta^{p-1}\#\{n:\mu_n>\delta\}\,d\delta.
+\]
+The strict count under the integral is bounded by the closed count \eqref{vfourD:countbound}. For any fixed $p>0$, $e^{40\sqrt{1+\log(1/\delta)}}\le C_p\delta^{-p/2}$ near zero, and $\delta^{p/2-1}$ is integrable. For $0<p<1$, $\mathfrak S_p$ denotes the usual Schatten quasi-class. Trace class does not imply finitely many or no negative eigenvalues.
+\end{proof}
